Hook-context parity follow-up — 2026-10-12 UTC:
- Pinned Java 5.5.1 `ConsumeMessageContext` leaves `accessChannel` unset until after the before-callback. Push initializes `props` to an empty map; Pull and Lite leave it null. The Rust context had initialized both fields for all consumer kinds.
- Updated Rust contexts to retain `None` for Pull/Lite props, use an empty map for Push, and set access channel after the before-callback and before the after-callback. The trace hook now reads optional props safely. Updated the existing three Push/Pull/Lite hook lifecycle tests to assert these source-derived values and timing.
- `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-consumer hook --locked` passed all 4 unit-hook tests plus the matching 3 integration hook tests (8 total); `cargo fmt --all -- --check` passed. The run reported one unused import, which was removed afterward; behavior and tests are unchanged by that import cleanup. Compatibility evidence was added to the Phase 16 row.
- Phase 16 remains open: the complete Java source/API/settings-to-test audit and final phase gates have not been completed.
- Correction to the test count above: `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-consumer hook --locked` ran 4 matching unit tests and 2 matching integration tests (6 total); the earlier count of 3 integration tests/8 total was mistaken.

NamespaceUtil public API completion — 2026-10-12 UTC:
- The pinned public `NamespaceUtil` surface includes constants plus one- and two-argument namespace removal, already-wrapped detection, retry wrapping, namespace extraction, retry/DLQ prefix removal and retry/DLQ predicates. Rust previously exposed only the ClientConfig wrap/unwrap paths. Added `rocketmq_classic_protocol::NamespaceUtil` and facade re-exports for every public operation/constant; ClientConfig adapters now delegate to it.
