- Expanded the pinned Java fixture and `namespace_util_fixture_matches_java_5_5_1` to compare all public utility operations/constants across normal, already-wrapped, retry/DLQ, system/custom system resources, empty/null, whitespace, multiple separators and Unicode cases. `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-core --test config namespace_util_fixture_matches_java_5_5_1 --locked`, `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-protocol namespace --locked` and `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-producer validation::tests --locked` passed. The Java fixture generator compiled and generated the updated fixture.
- Full source-to-test audit and Phase 16 acceptance remain open; this closes the identified NamespaceUtil method-surface omission only.

TLS client control-flow correction — 2026-10-12 UTC:
- Direct pinned-source review found that every Java `TlsHelper.buildSslContext(true)` branch returns before `moreTlsConfig`, so `tls.ciphers` and `tls.protocols` are loaded/overridden but do not affect the Java client handshake. Rust had begun selecting/rejecting these values in the client connector, which introduced behavior the Java client does not have.
- Rust now retains these property snapshots and ignores them in the client connector. The invoked Java TLS fixture sets invalid cipher/protocol values and builds a client context successfully to verify the client path. Updated the security guide and compatibility entry accordingly.
- `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-remoting --lib java_client_tls_ --locked` passed; `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-remoting --test security --locked` passed all 5 scripted negative cases; `mvn -f compat/java-reference/pom.xml verify` passed and validated all 32 manifest scenarios plus the TLS/namespace Java fixtures; `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` passed. Phase 16 remains open pending the complete feature audit and all-features gate.

Async producer hook/retry parity follow-up — 2026-10-12 UTC:
- Pinned-source review of `DefaultMQProducerImpl.sendKernelImpl` and `MQClientAPIImpl.sendMessageAsync` confirmed two Java ASYNC after-hook call sites: one after the async request submission returns, and another with the final success/error. The same `SendMessageContext` is retained through API-level retries, so before runs once. This corrects the earlier abbreviated audit note that described only the terminal after-hook.
- Rust Future send paths now prepare/compress once, retain the same hook context through retries, invoke after at submission and terminal completion, retry response/transport errors up to `retryTimesWhenSendAsyncFailed` except `TooManyRequests`, reroute default sends when route data is available, and retry explicit-queue/selector sends against the chosen Broker. Namespace restoration and unique IDs are retained across retries.
- Added scripted cases `async_send_retries_share_one_hook_context_until_terminal_result`, `explicit_queue_future_retries_on_the_same_broker_with_one_hook_lifecycle`, and `async_send_hooks_report_submission_and_terminal_failure_once_each`; updated retry-policy tests to reflect the pinned Java behavior. The first regression run failed as expected before implementation because a broker response error was returned without retrying.
- `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-producer --locked` passed: 85 unit tests, 3 producer integration tests, 3 request/reply tests, 9 scripted-send tests and 6 transaction tests. `cargo fmt --all -- --check` passed. `cargo clippy -p rocketmq-classic-producer --all-targets --all-features --locked -- -D warnings` passed after scoping test mutex guards.
- Phase 16 remains PARTIAL. The complete Java source/API/settings-to-test audit, final workspace/all-features gates, and phase acceptance/commit remain open; Phases 17 and 18 have not started, and Phase 6 reconciliation remains partial.

Phase 16 source audit — TopicValidator public surface, 2026-10-12 UTC:
- Pinned-source review of `common/.../topic/TopicValidator.java` found that the Rust facade exposed system-topic classification only; Java also publishes the character table, topic/group validation methods, and `ValidateResult`. The Rust producer validator also checked length before illegal characters and applied the normal 127-unit limit to retry/DLQ topics, unlike Java's validation order and 255-unit limit.
- Added `TopicValidatorFixtures` with constants, mutable character/system/send-block set behavior, exact validation remarks, null/blank inputs, ASCII and Unicode whitespace, Unicode rejection, UTF-16 length boundaries, illegal-before-length ordering, and retry/DLQ maximum lengths. Registered deterministic fixture drift verification and added `public_topic_validator_surface_matches_pinned_java_fixture`.
- Implemented the public Rust topic/group validation API with a mutable locked character table and Java-compatible remarks; producer validation now delegates to it. This fixes the retry/DLQ length and error precedence mismatches. `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-common --test topic_validator --locked` passed; `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-producer validation::tests --locked` passed; Java fixture generation via pinned Maven `compile exec:java` passed. The red differential initially failed to compile because the five omitted Rust operations were absent, confirming the surface gap.
- Added `PHASE_16_NAMESPACE_SECURITY_OPERATIONS_MATRIX.md` and linked it from the compatibility inventory. Phase 16 remains PARTIAL: ACL helper/API, hook, TLS, trace and event bidirectional surface audits are still open; final workspace/Maven/Clippy gates must rerun after the remaining changes. No Java checkout files were changed.

Phase 16 TopicValidator audit correction — 2026-10-12 UTC:
- A full producer-crate run showed that changing producer validation to delegate to `TopicValidator.validateTopic` broke two pinned producer fixture cases. Direct `TopicValidator.validateTopic` allows retry/DLQ names up to 255 units and checks illegal characters before length; `client/Validators.java` `checkTopic`, used by producer sends, instead caps every topic at 127 and checks length before illegal characters. These are distinct Java entry points and Rust must preserve both.
- Reverted the producer-validator delegation. The standalone `TopicValidator` API/fixture implementation remains; producer behavior stays on the existing Java `Validators.checkTopic` path. The producer integration assertions and `fixtures/producer/core.json` remain the source oracle for that separate path.
- Updated the Phase 16 matrix to map `Validators` independently. The common TopicValidator differential and producer regression suite are being rerun; the earlier log entry's claim that Producer behavior itself required the TopicValidator change is superseded by this correction.

